\details{
\subsection{Building the indicators data frame}{

Each row of \code{indicators} represents one survey indicator. The two key
decisions per row are:
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Type of indicator}: is it a proportion (binary variable like
"stunted yes/no") or a mean (continuous variable like "household
expenditure")? This determines whether you fill the \code{p} or \code{var}
column.
\item \strong{Precision target}: do you want an absolute margin of error
(\code{moe}, e.g. +/- 5 percentage points), the same margin stated
relative to the estimand (\code{rmoe}, e.g. 12 percent of the
proportion, as MICS and DHS state it), or a relative coefficient of
variation (\code{cv}, e.g. 10 percent relative error)?
}

A minimal example for three health indicators:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{indicators <- data.frame(
  name = c("stunting", "vaccination", "expenditure"),
  p    = c(0.30, 0.70, NA),
  var  = c(NA, NA, 2500),
  moe  = c(0.05, 0.05, 10)
)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Rows with \code{p} are treated as proportions, whereas rows with \code{var} (and
\code{p = NA}) are treated as means. You cannot have both \code{p} and \code{var} non-\code{NA}
in the same row.
}

\subsection{Column reference}{

\describe{
\item{\code{name}}{Indicator label (optional). If omitted, row numbers
are used in output.}
\item{\code{p}}{Expected proportion, in (0, 1). Use this for binary
indicators such as prevalences or coverage rates. The value is
your best prior guess (e.g. from a previous survey or literature).
One of \code{p} or \code{var} per row.}
\item{\code{var}}{Population variance of a continuous indicator. Use
this for means (e.g. income, expenditure, weight). One of \code{p}
or \code{var} per row.}
\item{\code{mu}}{Population mean (finite and non-zero; it may be
negative). It is required when \code{var} is used with \code{cv} because
CV = SE / abs(mean).}
\item{\code{moe}}{Margin of error, the half-width of the confidence
interval you want. For proportions, this is on the probability
scale (e.g. 0.05 for +/- 5 percentage points). For means,
it is in the same units as the variable (e.g. 10 dollars).}
\item{\code{rmoe}}{Margin of error relative to the row's estimand, so
0.12 asks for a half-width of 12 percent of \code{p} or of \code{abs(mu)}.
It is converted to \code{moe} on ingestion, so the row needs \code{p} or
\code{mu}, and it is read under the row's own \code{prop_method}.}
\item{\code{cv}}{Target coefficient of variation (relative standard
error). For example, 0.10 means the SE should be at most 10\\%
of the estimate.}
\item{\code{alpha}}{Significance level for the confidence interval
(default 0.05, giving a 95 percent CI).}
\item{\code{deff}}{Design effect multiplier (default 1). Set > 1 to inflate
the sample size for complex
designs (e.g. 1.5 for a cluster design).}
\item{\code{N}}{Population size (default \code{Inf}).
A finite value applies a finite population correction, reducing
the required sample size.}
\item{\code{prop_method}}{Proportion CI method:
\code{"wald"} (default), \code{"wilson"}, \code{"logodds"}, or \code{"beta"}.
\code{"wilson"} is recommended for rare proportions (below 0.1 or above
0.9), and \code{"beta"} (Korn-Graubard) when the expected number of
positive counts is small enough that the interval should stay
inside \verb{[0, 1]} by construction. It is used only for rows with
\code{p}. See \code{\link[=n_prop]{n_prop()}} for how to choose.}
\item{\code{df}}{Degrees of freedom of the planned variance estimator,
typically sampled PSUs minus strata, and available from
\code{\link[=design_df]{design_df()}}. It switches that row's interval quantile from normal
to t, under every method; \code{NA} (the default) applies no adjustment.
A df is a property of the design rather than of an indicator, so
every row of a single-domain table shares one value. The column
earns its place when the rows are \emph{domains}, each covering its own
set of strata: \code{design_df(alloc)$domains$.df} gives one value per
domain to match in.}
\item{\code{min_cases}}{Minimum expected number of positive cases the row
must yield, a floor on that row's own size, as in
\code{\link[=n_prop]{n_prop()}}\verb{(min_cases = )}. Proportion rows only; \code{NA} (the
default) sizes the row on precision alone. The row that ends up
largest is still the binding one, whichever constraint raised it.
Single-stage \code{n_multi()} only: a multistage design sizes stages
against a cost and has no single total for a count to raise.}
\item{\code{unit_relvar}}{Unit relvariance. If omitted, derived
automatically from \code{p} (as \code{(1 - p) / p}) or from
\code{var} / \code{mu^2}.}
\item{\code{resp_rate}}{Expected response rate at the ultimate unit, in
(0, 1]. Default 1 (no adjustment). A value of 0.90 inflates the
sample size by \code{1 / 0.90} to compensate for 10 percent
non-response. It means the same thing in \code{\link[=n_multi_cluster]{n_multi_cluster()}},
which additionally takes \code{resp_rate_psu} for whole clusters that
cannot be worked and \code{resp_rate_ssu} for second-stage units in a
three-stage design. Naming a stage the design does not have is an
error rather than a column carried along and ignored.}
}

Domain columns are specified via the \code{domains} parameter. When domains
are present, sizing runs independently for each domain combination, and
\code{domain_sampling} decides how those requirements combine into the one
number \code{n} reports. The two readings answer different questions and give
different sizes, so the choice belongs to the design rather than to a
default: separate quotas need every requirement met and therefore their
sum, while natural domains need a sample large enough that the rarest
demanding domain turns up often enough. Neither is the largest single
requirement, which \code{n_domain_max} reports separately.

Columns beyond those listed are carried along and ignored, so the table
can keep questionnaire modules, sources, or other bookkeeping. The
exception is a name the rest of the package would lead you to expect
here: \code{mean} (this table takes \code{mu}, while the \code{\link[=n_alloc]{n_alloc()}} frame takes
\code{mean}), \code{indicator} or \code{label} (it takes \code{name}), \code{method} (it takes
\code{prop_method}), and \code{icc} or \code{var_ratio} (which are per stage here, so
\code{icc_psu} and \code{var_ratio_psu}). Those are rejected with the name they
should have carried rather than silently ignored. A column named
through \code{domains} is exempt, so a domain may be called \code{label} or
\code{method}.

The dispersion of a continuous indicator may be given as \code{var} or as
\code{sd}, whichever the source reports, exactly as in \code{\link[=n_mean]{n_mean()}}. They are
different quantities rather than two names for one, so supplying both
in the same table is an error rather than a preference; \code{sd} is squared
on the way in and everything downstream reads \code{var}.

\code{n_multi()} computes sample size per indicator by delegating proportion
rows to \code{\link[=n_prop]{n_prop()}} and mean rows to \code{\link[=n_mean]{n_mean()}},
then takes the maximum per domain. Use \code{prop_method} or a
\code{indicators$prop_method} column to choose \code{"wald"}, \code{"wilson"},
\code{"logodds"} or \code{"beta"} for proportion rows.
}
}
\examples{
# Simple mode: three indicators, take the max
indicators <- data.frame(
  name = c("stunting", "vaccination", "anemia"),
  p    = c(0.30, 0.70, 0.10),
  moe  = c(0.05, 0.05, 0.03)
)
n_multi(indicators)

# MICS/DHS-style: state precision as a relative margin of error
targets_rmoe <- data.frame(
  name = c("stunting", "vaccination", "anemia"),
  p    = c(0.30, 0.70, 0.10),
  rmoe = 0.12,
  deff = c(2.0, 1.5, 2.5)
)
n_multi(targets_rmoe)

# Continuous indicators: 'var' for the dispersion, 'mu' for the mean.
# A CV target needs 'mu', because a relative standard error is relative
# to something; a MOE target does not.
targets_mean <- data.frame(
  name = c("expenditure", "hh_size"),
  var  = c(250000, 4.0),
  mu   = c(1200, 5.4),
  cv   = c(0.05, 0.03)
)
n_multi(targets_mean)

# Proportions and means in one table, sized to a common CV
targets_both <- data.frame(
  name = c("stunting", "expenditure"),
  p    = c(0.30, NA),
  var  = c(NA, 250000),
  mu   = c(NA, 1200),
  cv   = c(0.08, 0.05)
)
n_multi(targets_both)

# Rare proportion: use Wilson globally in simple mode
n_multi(indicators[3, , drop = FALSE], prop_method = "wilson")

# Korn-Graubard for an indicator whose expected count is small, with the
# degrees of freedom of the planned variance estimator. 'df' is read on
# beta rows only, so the other rows carry NA.
targets_rare <- data.frame(
  name = c("stunting", "cocaine_use"),
  p    = c(0.30, 0.02),
  moe  = c(0.05, 0.01),
  prop_method = c("wald", "beta"),
  df   = c(NA, 25)
)
n_multi(targets_rare)

# Per-row proportion methods in a mixed target table
targets_mixed <- data.frame(
  name = c("rare_prop", "mean_ind"),
  p = c(0.05, NA),
  var = c(NA, 100),
  moe = c(0.02, 2),
  prop_method = c("wilson", NA)
)
n_multi(targets_mixed)

# Simple mode with domains
targets_dom <- data.frame(
  name   = rep(c("stunting", "anemia"), each = 2),
  p      = c(0.30, 0.25, 0.10, 0.15),
  moe    = c(0.05, 0.05, 0.03, 0.03),
  region = rep(c("North", "South"), 2)
)
n_multi(targets_dom, domains = "region")

# Two-stage CV mode
targets_cl <- data.frame(
  name   = c("stunting", "anemia"),
  p      = c(0.30, 0.10),
  cv     = c(0.10, 0.15),
  icc_psu = c(0.02, 0.05)
)
n_multi_cluster(targets_cl, stage_cost = c(500, 50))

# Two-stage with MOE (converted to CV internally)
targets_moe <- data.frame(
  name   = c("stunting", "anemia"),
  p      = c(0.30, 0.10),
  moe    = c(0.05, 0.03),
  icc_psu = c(0.02, 0.05)
)
n_multi_cluster(targets_moe, stage_cost = c(500, 50))

# Joint budget allocation across domains
targets_jnt <- data.frame(
  name   = rep(c("stunting", "anemia"), each = 2),
  p      = c(0.30, 0.25, 0.10, 0.15),
  cv     = c(0.10, 0.10, 0.15, 0.15),
  icc_psu = c(0.02, 0.03, 0.05, 0.04),
  region = rep(c("Urban", "Rural"), 2)
)
n_multi_cluster(
  targets_jnt,
  stage_cost = c(500, 50),
  domains = "region",
  budget = 100000,
  allocation = "joint"
)

}
